   <section class='publication-abstract'><h2 class='publication-abstract-heading'>Abstract</h2><div class='publication-abstract-body'>Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC) controls forgetting with a quadratic
penalty, yet its empirical Fisher is usually justified through probabilistic
curvature. We formulate <span class='pplri7t-x-x-109'>Continual Learning as an Optimization Constraint </span>(CLOC),
preserving chosen functionals of previously learned behavior. First-order
preservation of any differentiable scalar functional yields a gradient outer
product; for per-sample negative log likelihood, it is exactly EWC’s empirical
Fisher, without a model-Fisher interpretation. Our central method, Improved
Elastic Weight Consolidation (IEWC), instead preserves outputs modulo
motions tangent to old loss level sets, separating EWC’s implicit loss-gradient
weighting from parameter sensitivity. IEWC is specified by <span class='mathjax-inline'>\\((\\lambda ,\\kappa ,\\Gamma ,h,\\Pi )\\)</span>: preservation
strength, relative damping, output geometry, sample weights, and matrix
approximation. A contamination experiment confirms that normalization
prevents corrupt samples from dominating the full matrix. Across five domains,
IEWC variants improve EWC’s selected means in classification, segmentation,
and language, while remaining comparable in forecasting and diffusion. In a
validation-selected locked study, uniform IEWC improves ImageNet-R final
average accuracy over matched EWC by 2.00 percentage points (95% CI
0.93–3.07) and gives no material mean difference on TRACE (<span class='mathjax-inline'>\\(-0.11\\)</span>; 95% CI
<span class='mathjax-inline'>\\(-1.48\\)</span>–<span class='mathjax-inline'>\\(1.27\\)</span>).</div></section>
